diff -ruN a/docs/avx-emulation.md b/docs/avx-emulation.md
--- a/docs/avx-emulation.md	1970-01-01 00:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ b/docs/avx-emulation.md	2026-10-31 10:53:34.379954555 +0000
@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@
+# AVX / SSE4.2 / POPCNT emulation (CPUs older than Sandy Bridge)
+
+The game code is built for the PS4's Jaguar CPU and uses AVX (128 and 256 bit), F16C, POPCNT and
+BMI1, thousands of times each. A host without them (Core 2, e.g. Q8400) raises an illegal
+instruction at each one. `src/avx_emulate.c` emulates them from the fault handler (`probe.c`):
+the instruction is decoded, executed, and the thread resumes after it.
+
+* On by default when the CPU lacks AVX, SSE4.2 or POPCNT. `BB_AVX_EMULATE=0` restores the old STOP.
+* Handled: the VEX forms (scalar and packed, 128 bit, and every 256 bit form that exists on Jaguar,
+  including the width-changing vcvt* and vmovmskps/vptest ymm), F16C, AES, PCLMUL, BMI1
+  (andn/bextr/blsi/blsr/blsmsk), vldmxcsr/vstmxcsr, vcmp predicates 0-31, and the SSE4.2 set:
+  CRC32, PCMPGTQ, PCMPESTRI/PCMPESTRM/PCMPISTRI/PCMPISTRM (legacy and VEX), plus POPCNT and MOVBE.
+* Still not covered: LZCNT/TZCNT (they run as BSR/BSF without a fault, so they cannot be trapped;
+  scripts/isa_rewrite.py rewrites them, which needs `pip install iced-x86`) and the 256 bit integer
+  AVX2 forms (vpaddd ymm...), which Jaguar does not have and eboot.bin does not use as code.
+  Every distinct AVX/POPCNT/SSE4.2 encoding found in eboot.bin is covered. An instruction that still
+  fails prints `AVX emulation stopped: <reason>: <bytes>`.
+* How: a VEX instruction that has a legacy SSE twin is rebuilt as that instruction and run natively
+  on fixed registers, so the host CPU computes the result; the ymm upper halves live in a per-thread
+  shadow. Instructions with no twin (vinsertf128, vpermilps, vbroadcast*, vmaskmov*, vtestps, F16C...)
+  are plain C.
+* Batching: one fault emulates the whole straight run of VEX instructions that follows (up to 128).
+  In eboot.bin the 573k AVX instructions sit in 153k runs (average 3.7, 35% of them in runs of 17+),
+  so this cuts the number of faults several-fold. Verified on random runs against real AVX hardware.
+* Cost: one exception dispatch per fault (microseconds) for the sites left to the fault handler; a
+  rewritten site (below) costs a stub call instead. Expect a low frame rate; the point is to get the
+  game running.
+* Windows caveat, and what removes it: the guest uses the System V red zone (128 bytes below rsp) and
+  the Windows exception dispatcher does not preserve it, so an AVX instruction that faults inside a leaf
+  function with live red-zone data corrupts that data (a null table pointer in an LZMA decoder was the
+  first symptom). Linux (SIGILL) is unaffected. Hence the AVX stubs:
+
+## AVX stubs (no exception, red zone safe)
+
+`scripts/isa_rewrite.py --avx` (run.py passes it when the CPU needs the emulation) adds one `AVX`
+record per VEX instruction to `isa-rewrite.bin`: 5+ byte instructions get a `jmp` to a stub, 4-byte
+ones a `jmp` that also covers the plain instruction after them (copied into the stub); 3-byte ones
+(`vzeroupper`) and 4-byte ones without a plain successor stay with the fault handler. A stub does
+`lea rsp,[rsp-0x80]`, saves rax and calls `avx_stub_common` (src/avx_stub.c), which saves all
+registers, the flags, xmm0-15 and mxcsr, calls `avx_stub_dispatch` (src/probe.c) and restores them;
+the instruction is decoded from the record's `original` bytes (`avx_emulate_at`), the site itself
+holds the `jmp`. No exception, so the red zone is never written.
+
+* The table gets large (about 570000 records, 64 bytes each, plus 64-byte stubs); load time is a
+  second or two.
+* `BB_ISA_REWRITE=0` ignores the AVX records (fault handler only).
+* The launch log says `ISA: N AVX sites rewritten to stubs`. Sites that are not rewritten (the cases
+  above, or bytes changed by a patch) are emulated at fault time as before.
+* Not covered: a computed jump that lands on the instruction after a 4-byte site (the same
+  limitation as the BMI1 relocation) and `vzeroupper` in a leaf function that keeps data in the red
+  zone after it.
+
+## Test (needs a CPU with AVX, F16C, AES, PCLMUL, BMI1; Linux)
+
+    python3 scripts/avx_samples.py eboot.bin samples.txt
+    gcc -std=gnu11 -O1 -mno-red-zone tests/test_avx_emulate.c -o out/avx-test
+    out/avx-test samples.txt 3
+
+Every encoding runs natively and through the emulator on the same random registers and memory;
+registers, memory and flags are compared.
+
+## Test of the stubs (a CPU with AVX; Linux)
+
+    gcc -std=gnu11 -O1 -mno-red-zone -Isrc tests/test_avx_stub.c src/avx_emulate.c src/avx_stub.c \
+        src/isa_rewrite.c src/isa_emulate.c -pthread -o out/avx-stub-test && out/avx-stub-test
+
+Runs a function that keeps live data in the red zone and uses AVX (4-, 5- and 8-byte sites,
+rip-relative, flags, mxcsr) natively and rewritten, on the same input; everything must be identical,
+also with four threads.
